<!--
    SPDX-FileCopyrightText: Copyright (c) Trinix
    SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0

    Something on the screen that is unmistakably text, and two controls that are
    unmistakably not primitives.

    ⚠ **This file replaces a C# `Greeting` whose remark was wrong, and saying which part
    was wrong is the point of this comment.** That remark said markup was avoided because
    it "brings the VXML compiler and the generated utility stylesheet", so a first
    application proving a window works should not pay for them. Neither half was true even
    when it was written. `Vixen.Ui.Markup.Generators.dll` travels inside the `Vixen.Ui`
    package's `analyzers/dotnet/cs/`, and `buildTransitive/Vixen.Ui.targets` — transitive
    on purpose, because nobody references `Vixen.Ui` by name — supplies the `**/*.vxml`
    glob that makes a file like this compiler input. `Vixen.Ui.Styling.Utilities` was
    likewise already in the closure. Referencing `Vixen.Ui.Desktop` had brought both since
    the first pin, so writing this in markup costs nothing that was not already paid.

    ⚠ **The `Vixen.Ui.Markup` package is a different thing from the VXML compiler, and
    that is the confusion worth not repeating.** The package is the parser, binder and
    emitter as a *runtime* library, for tooling and hot reload. The compiler is the
    generator inside `Vixen.Ui`. Pinning the package does not make markup compile, and not
    pinning it never stopped it — see `docs/plan/01-system-sdk.md` § "What already exists".

    ⚠ **The `DataGrid` and the `TreeView` are the half that did need a new package**, and they
    are here rather than in a test because a control that constructs is not a control
    that draws: these are mounted in the document the Phase 5 graphics gate presents,
    so a virtualiser that faults on lavapipe faults where somebody is watching.
    Deliberately three rows and four nodes — doc 07's Files and doc 11's System Monitor
    are what the controls are *for*; this is what proves they are reachable.
-->
@component Greeting
@namespace Trinix.Apps.HelloUi
@tag greeting
@using Vixen.Ui
@using Vixen.Ui.Controls.Advanced

<div>
    <span>Hello from Vixen, on Trinix.</span>
    <span>Rendered by lavapipe, presented through wl_shm.</span>

    <TreeView ref="@chain" />
    <DataGrid ref="@links" />
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>One link of the chain this application exists to prove.</summary>
    /// <param name="Layer">Where in the stack it sits.</param>
    /// <param name="Provider">What implements it.</param>
    sealed record Link(string Layer, string Provider);

    TreeView chain = null!;
    DataGrid links = null!;

    /// <summary>Fills the two controls, which is the whole of what they are here to do.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    ///     ⚠ <b>Both are filled in code rather than nested in the markup above, and that is
    ///     Vixen's design rather than a shortcut.</b> A virtualiser realises what fits the
    ///     viewport, so its contents are a model and not an element per row; the sample's own
    ///     `Hierarchy.vxml` says the same thing at length. What markup buys here is the
    ///     mounting, the `ref`s and the tag — which is exactly what an application's real
    ///     views buy from it.
    /// </remarks>
    partial void OnComposed() {
        var compositor = chain.Root.Add("trinix-compositor");
        var client = compositor.Add("trinix-wl-client");

        client.Add("TrinixPlatform");
        client.Add("lavapipe");

        chain.Refresh();
        chain.Expand(compositor);

        links.AddColumn("Layer", item => ((Link)item).Layer);
        links.AddColumn("Provider", item => ((Link)item).Provider);
        links.SetItems([
            new Link("Display", "trinix-compositor"),
            new Link("Device", "lavapipe"),
            new Link("Present", "wl_shm")
        ]);
    }
}
